.TH voltkit-awake 1  "Voltkit 0.2.2" "Voltkit Manual"
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NAME
voltkit\-awake \- Keep the computer from sleeping or locking its screen
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvoltkit awake\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fIDURATION\fR] 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH DESCRIPTION
Keep the computer from sleeping or locking its screen
.PP
Held by the background service, so it continues after this command or the window closes. Without DURATION, shows whether Keep Awake is on.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
[\fIDURATION\fR]
30m, 1h, 1h30m or a number of minutes; `on` until turned off; `off` to stop
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NOTES
.PP
The background service holds the request through the desktop\*(Aqs Inhibit portal, so it needs `voltkit daemon` running. When the time is up, Keep Awake ends by itself and shows a notification.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Stay awake for a 90\-minute movie:
.RS 4
.nf
\fBvoltkit awake 1h30m\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Stay awake until told otherwise:
.RS 4
.nf
\fBvoltkit awake on\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check, then stop:
.RS 4
.nf
\fBvoltkit awake
voltkit awake off\fR
.fi
.RE
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH "SEE ALSO"
